<section>
<h3 class="c-head" epub:type="title">Background Knowledge</h3>
<p class="noindent">Read to learn about important terms you’ll see in <i>The Last Cuentista.</i></p>
<ul class="bullets" role="list">
<li class="bulls"><span aria-hidden="true">•</span><span class="spaceb"><span class="sr-only"> </span></span>A <b>comet</b> is an object made of dust and ice that orbits, or travels around, the sun. Comets look like they have cloudy tails. People have told stories about comets for over 2,000 years. One of the most famous comets is <b>Halley’s Comet</b>, which can be seen from Earth every 75 to 76 years.</li>
<li class="bulls"><span aria-hidden="true">•</span><span class="spaceb"><span class="sr-only"> </span></span>A <b>nagual</b> (NAH-wal) is a mythical being or animal spirit that acts as a protector or trickster. Naguals are found in traditional stories from Mexico and Central America. In some stories, a nagual can “shapeshift” into different animals.</li>
</ul>
<figure>
<p class="imagecap"><img alt="" class="w50" role="presentation" src="../images/p12.jpg" aria-hidden="true"/></p>
</figure>
<div class="top1"/>
<div class="gray-g">
<p class="caption">Halley’s Comet is about 9 miles (15 km) long and 5 miles (8 km) wide.</p>
</div>
<div class="box-talk">
<p class="box-head-talk"><span class="box-do-talk">Talk About It</span></p>
<p class="noindenttalk">Discuss these questions with your partner.</p>
<ul class="bullets" role="list">
<li class="bulls"><span aria-hidden="true">•</span><span class="spaceb"><span class="sr-only"> </span></span>Why do you think people have told stories about comets for so long?</li>
<li class="bulls"><span aria-hidden="true">•</span><span class="spaceb"><span class="sr-only"> </span></span>What do you think a nagual might look like? Why?</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="speech-bubble">
<p class="noindent-comic">I think people told stories about comets because<span aria-label="blank" role="img">_____</span>.</p>
</div>
<div class="top"/>
<div class="block"/>
<div class="right">
<div class="speech-bubble-right">
<p class="noindent-comic">I think a nagual looks like <span aria-label="blank" role="img">_____</span> because <span aria-label="blank" role="img">_____</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div aria-hidden="true"><span aria-hidden="true" aria-label="13" epub:type="pagebreak" id="page_13" role="doc-pagebreak"/></div>
<p class="from"><b>from</b></p>
<p class="b-head-p" id="sec2_2"><b>The Last Cuentista</b></p>
<p class="byline"><b>by Donna Barba Higuera</b></p>
<div class="box-b">
<p class="box-head_B">Purpose for Reading</p>
<p class="noindent-blu">Read to learn how Petra and her grandmother are preparing for Petra’s journey.</p>
</div>
<figure>
<p class="imagec"><img alt="" class="w100" role="presentation" src="../images/MLR04_U02_p013_001_epub.jpg" aria-hidden="true"/></p>
</figure>
<p class="poemintro"><i>The year is 2061. Scientists predict that, for the first time, Halley’s Comet will collide with Earth. Twelve-year-old Petra Peña and her family have been chosen to board a spaceship to the planet Sagan before the comet hits. Petra must say goodbye to Lita, her beloved grandmother, in New Mexico.</i></p>
<ol class="poemlist" role="list">
<li class="poem"><span class="gray">1</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>Lita tosses another <span lang="es" xml:lang="es">piñon</span> log onto the fire. Sweet smoke drifts past us into the starry sky. Her knees crack as she sits back down on the blanket next to me. The cup of hot chocolate with cinnamon she’s made me sits untouched this time.</li>
<li><p class="poem"><span class="gray">2</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“I have something I want you to take with you on your trip, Petra.” Lita reaches into her sweater pocket. “Since I won’t be there for your thirteenth birthday . . .” She holds out a silver pendant in the shape of a sun. Its center is filled with a flat black stone. “If you hold it up to the sun, its light glows through the <span class="bluep">obsidian</span>.”</p>
<div class="box-voc">
<p class="noindent"><b>obsidian:</b> <i>dark, glassy volcanic rock formed from lava</i></p>
</div></li>
<li class="poem"><span class="gray">3</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>I take it from her hand and hold it up, but there’s no sun. Only the moon. Sometimes I try to imagine I can see things I really can’t. But I’m sure a faint glow filters through the middle of the stone. I move the pendant back and forth. It disappears completely when I move it too far from the center of my vision.</li>
<li class="poem"><span aria-hidden="true" aria-label="14" epub:type="pagebreak" id="page_14" role="doc-pagebreak"/><span class="gray">4</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>When I look back, Lita is motioning to an <b>identical</b> pendant around her neck. “You know,” she says, “Yucatecos believe obsidian holds magic. A doorway to bring lost ones together.” She purses her lips. Her brown skin wrinkles toward her nose like cracked bark on a tree.</li>
<li class="poem"><span class="gray">5</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“They shouldn’t force me to go,” I say.</li>
<li class="poem"><span class="gray">6</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“You have to, Petra.” Lita looks away for a long time before speaking again. “Children are not meant to be separated from their parents.”</li>
<li class="poem"><span class="gray">7</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“You’re Dad’s parent. He should stay with you then. We all should.” Even as I say it, I know I sound like a little kid.</li>
<li><p class="poem"><span class="gray">8</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>She laughs a deep, soft chuckle. “I’m too old to travel so far. But for you . . . <span class="bluep" lang="es" xml:lang="es">Dios mío</span>, a new planet! How exciting.”</p>
<div class="box-voc">
<p class="noindent"><b><span lang="es" xml:lang="es">Dios mío</span>:</b> <i>oh my god</i></p>
</div></li>
<li class="poem"><span class="gray">9</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>My chin trembles and I bury my head into her side, squeezing her around the waist.</li>
<li><p class="poem1"><span class="gray">10</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“I don’t want to leave you.”</p>
<figure>
<p class="imagec"><img alt="" class="w100" role="presentation" src="../images/MLR04_U02_p014_001_epub.jpg" aria-hidden="true"/></p>
</figure></li>
<li class="poem1"><span aria-hidden="true" aria-label="15" epub:type="pagebreak" id="page_15" role="doc-pagebreak"/><span class="gray">11</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>Her stomach lowers with a deep sigh. Somewhere off in the desert behind Lita’s house, a coyote howls, calling for its friends. As if on cue, the chickens cluck, and one of her fainting goats bleats.</li>
</ol>
<div class="box-unit-Vs">
<p class="box-head-vss">Stop and Discuss</p>
<p class="noindent-Bsd"><b>Why is Petra upset?</b><span class="block"/>Underline details in paragraphs 4–10 that help you answer the question.</p>
</div>
<ol class="poemlist" role="list">
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">12</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“You need a <span lang="es" xml:lang="es">cuento</span>,” she says, referring to one of her tall tales.</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">13</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>We lie back looking up at the night sky. The warm desert wind blows over us as Lita pulls me into the tightest hug ever. I never want to leave this spot.</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">14</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>She points up at Halley’s Comet. From here, it doesn’t look so dangerous.</li>
<li><p class="poem1"><span class="gray">15</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“<span class="bluep" lang="es" xml:lang="es">Había una vez</span>,” she begins her story, “a young fire snake nagual. His mother was Earth, his father the sun.”</p>
<div class="box-voc">
<p class="noindent"><b><span lang="es" xml:lang="es">Había una vez</span>:</b> <i>“once upon a time” in Spanish</i></p>
</div></li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">16</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“A nagual snake?” I ask. “But how can the sun and Earth be parents to something part human, part animal—”</li>
<li><p class="poem1"><span class="gray">17</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“<i>Sssh</i>. This is my story.” She clears her throat and takes one of my hands in hers. “Fire Snake was angry. His mother, Earth, fed and nurtured him, but his father, the sun, stayed away. His father would bring crops, but he also brought great <span class="bluep">drought</span> and death. One very hot day as Sun loomed over the nagual,” Lita waves her arms toward the heavens, “he challenged his father. Even though his mother begged him to stay with her forever, the young Fire Snake sped off toward his father.”</p>
<div class="box-voc">
<p class="noindent"><b>drought:</b> <i>a long period without rain</i></p>
</div></li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">18</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>Lita remains silent for a moment. I know the stall is part of her strategy to keep me in suspense. It works.</li>
<li class="poem1"><span aria-hidden="true" aria-label="16" epub:type="pagebreak" id="page_16" role="doc-pagebreak"/><span class="gray">19</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“Then what?”</li>
<li><p class="poem1"><span class="gray">20</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>She smiles and continues. “With his tail flaming behind him, Fire Snake gained speed until he could not slow himself. But as he approached his father, Sun, he realized his mistake. His father’s flames were far more powerful and stronger than anything else in this universe. The nagual looped around his father, speeding back toward his home, but it was all too late. His father’s fire had burned his eyes, so he could no longer see.” Lita clicks her tongue. “<span lang="es" xml:lang="es">Pobrecito</span>, blinded and moving so fast he could never slow down. Never able to find his mother.” She sighs. Now comes the part in all her stories where her voice becomes lighter, like she’s casually giving directions to the corner <span class="bluep">panadería</span>. “So, every seventy-five years, he retraces the journey, hoping to reunite with her.” She points again at the fire snake. “Close enough to sense his mother, but never to <b>embrace.</b>”</p>
<div class="box-voc">
<p class="noindent"><b>panadería:</b> <i>bakery</i></p>
</div></li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">21</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“Except this time,” I say, heat running up my back.</li>
<li><p class="poem1"><span class="gray">22</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“Yes,” she answers, pulling me closer. “In a few days, the fire snake will finally find his mother. Y colorín Colorado, <span class="bluep" lang="es" xml:lang="es">este cuento se ha acabado,</span>” she says, ending her <span lang="es" xml:lang="es">cuento</span>.</p>
<div class="box-voc">
<p class="noindent"><b><span lang="es" xml:lang="es">este cuento se ha acabado</span>:</b> <i>”this story is over” in Spanish</i></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<div class="box-unit-Vs">
<p class="box-head-vss">Stop and Discuss</p>
<p class="noindent-Bsd"><b>Why does Lita tell the story of the nagual? What does it represent?</b><span class="block"/>Underline details in paragraphs 20–22 that help you answer the question.</p>
</div>
<ol class="poemlist" role="list">
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">23</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>I rub her hand over and over, memorizing her wrinkles. “Who told you that story? Your grandma?”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">24</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>Lita shrugs. “She told me bits. I might have made most of it up.”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">25</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“I’m scared, Lita,” I whisper.</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">26</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>She pats my arm. “But for a moment, did you forget your troubles?”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">27</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>I don’t answer out of shame. Her story had made me forget. Forget about what could happen to her and everyone else.</li>
<li class="poem1"><span aria-hidden="true" aria-label="17" epub:type="pagebreak" id="page_17" role="doc-pagebreak"/><span class="gray">28</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“Don’t you be afraid,” she says. “I’m not. It’s only the nagual coming home.”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">29</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>I glance up at Fire Snake in silence. “I’m going to be just like you, Lita. A storyteller.”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">30</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>She sits up, legs crossed, facing me. “A storyteller, yes. It’s in your blood.” She leans in. “But just like me? No, <span lang="es" xml:lang="es">mija</span>. You need to discover who you are and be that.”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">31</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“What if I ruin your stories?” I ask.</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">32</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>Lita cups my chin in her soft, brown hand. “You can’t ruin them. They’ve traveled hundreds of years, and through many people to find you. Now, go make them your own.”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">33</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>I think of Lita and her mother, and her mother’s mother. How much they knew. Who am I to follow them?</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">34</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>I clutch the pendant in my hand. “I’ll never lose your stories, Lita.”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">35</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>“You know, the planet you are going to will have a sun or two also.” She taps her pendant with her fingernail. “Look for me when you arrive?”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">36</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>My lower lip quivers, and tears fall down my face. “I can’t believe we’re leaving you.”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">37</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>She wipes a tear from my cheek. “It’s impossible for you to leave me. I’m part of you. You’re taking me and my stories to a new planet and hundreds of years into the future. How lucky I am.”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">38</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>I kiss her cheek. “I promise to make you proud.”</li>
<li class="poem1"><span class="gray">39</span><span class="sr-only">:</span><span class="spacep1"/>Gripping my obsidian pendant, I wonder if Lita will watch the fire snake through the smoky glass, when he finally reunites with his mother.</li>
</ol>
<div class="box-unit-Vs">
<p class="box-head-vss">Stop and Discuss</p>
<p class="noindent-Bsd"><b>How does Lita’s story affect Petra?</b><span class="block"/>Underline details in paragraphs 27–38 that help you answer the question.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section>
<div aria-hidden="true"><span aria-hidden="true" aria-label="18" epub:type="pagebreak" id="page_18" role="doc-pagebreak"/></div>
<h2 class="b-head" epub:type="title" id="sec2_3">Make Inferences</h2>
<p class="noindent">Sometimes a reader has to make an inference to understand ideas or information an author doesn’t state directly. An <b>inference</b> is an idea you figure out using clues from the text and your own knowledge about the topic or situation.</p>
<p class="noindentt">Make an inference to answer each question. First, think about the text evidence and what it tells you. Next, write what you already know. Then write an inference that answers the question.</p>
<ol class="order" role="list">
<li><p class="numberm"><b>1.<span class="spaceb"><span class="sr-only"> </span></span>Why does Lita tell Petra a story?</b></p>
<figure>
<p class="imagec"><img alt="" class="w100" role="presentation" src="../images/p18.jpg" aria-hidden="true"/></p>
</figure></li>
<li><p class="number"><span aria-hidden="true" aria-label="19" epub:type="pagebreak" id="page_19" role="doc-pagebreak"/><b>2.<span class="spaceb"><span class="sr-only"> </span></span>What is the meaning behind the nagual/Fire Snake story?</b></p>
<figure>
<p class="imagec"><img alt="" class="w100" role="presentation" src="../images/p19.jpg" aria-hidden="true"/></p>
</figure></li>
</ol>
<div class="box-talk">
<p class="box-head-talk"><span class="box-do-talk">Talk About It</span></p>
<p class="noindenttalk">Discuss your inferences with a partner.</p>
</div>
<div class="speech-bubble">
<p class="noindent-comic">I think Lita’s story is about <span aria-label="blank" role="img">_____</span> because <span aria-label="blank" role="img">_____</span>.</p>
</div>
<div class="top"/>
<div class="block"/>
<div class="right">
<div class="speech-bubble-right">
<p class="noindent-comic">I can tell that the snake nagual represents <span aria-label="blank" role="img">_____</span> because <span aria-label="blank" role="img">_____</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section>
<div aria-hidden="true"><span aria-hidden="true" aria-label="20" epub:type="pagebreak" id="page_20" role="doc-pagebreak"/></div>
<h2 class="b-head" epub:type="title" id="sec2_4">How Words Work</h2>
<section>
<h3 class="c-headt" epub:type="title">Context Clues</h3>
<p class="noindent"><b>Context clues</b> are words, phrases, or sentences near an unfamiliar word that can help you figure out the word’s meaning. There are different types of context clues.</p>
<ul class="bullets" role="list">
<li class="bulls"><span aria-hidden="true">•</span><span class="spaceb"><span class="sr-only"> </span></span><b>Synonyms</b> are words that have the same or similar meanings.</li>
<li class="bulls"><span aria-hidden="true">•</span><span class="spaceb"><span class="sr-only"> </span></span>An <b>example</b> shows what something is like.</li>
</ul>
<p class="noindentt">Find each word in the text. Look for context clues around the word. Then write the meaning of the word.</p>
<table class="table_b">
<colgroup>
<col class="w33"/>
<col class="w34"/>
<col class="w33"/>
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr>
<th class="tdb_1r" scope="col"><p class="tabhead"><b>Word</b></p></th>
<th class="tdb_1r" scope="col"><p class="tabhead"><b>Context Clues</b></p></th>
<th class="tdb_1" scope="col"><p class="tabhead"><b>Meaning</b></p></th>
</tr>
</thead>
